msgstr ""

#: src/Infrastructure/WordPress/Admin/ListTable/ListScreenSetup.php:181
msgid "Once every redirect has been checked, by the background check or <strong>Validate all redirects</strong>, a <strong>Has issues</strong> filter shows only the redirects the check flagged, so bulk actions can be applied straight to them. The filter is a snapshot labeled with when the last full check finished: editing a redirect clears its flag until it is next checked. See the Validate and Test help tab for what validation can and cannot see."
msgstr ""

#: src/Infrastructure/WordPress/Admin/ListTable/ListScreenSetup.php:193
msgid "There are two ways to check redirects, and they look at different evidence:"
msgstr ""

#: src/Infrastructure/WordPress/Admin/ListTable/ListScreenSetup.php:195
msgid "<strong>Validate all redirects</strong> (the toolbar button) checks the <em>stored details</em> of every redirect, enabled and disabled, in batches. Validation never requests a URL, so it is safe to run at any size, but it cannot see problems only a live request reveals. The same check runs in the background, a few seconds every five minutes, so every redirect is checked regularly without anyone pressing the button; the line beside the button says when the last full check finished and how long one takes."
msgstr ""

#: src/Infrastructure/WordPress/Admin/ListTable/ListScreenSetup.php:196
msgid "<strong>Test</strong> (the row action, also available as a bulk action) checks <em>one redirect live</em>: it runs every stored-data check, requests the destination over HTTP, and, unless the redirect is disabled, requests the source to confirm the redirect actually fires. Test results appear in the Health column and are not stored, because a timeout says nothing definite about a redirect."
msgstr ""

#. translators: 1: a WP-CLI command, wp legacy-redirector test; 2: a WP-CLI command, wp legacy-redirector validate. Do not translate the commands.
#: src/Infrastructure/WordPress/Admin/ListTable/ListScreenSetup.php:200
#, php-format
msgid "So a clean validation does not prove a redirect works end to end - a destination page can still be down, and only a Test (or %1$s) can see that. On the command line, %2$s and %1$s do the same two jobs. A <strong>problem</strong> means the redirect is broken and will not do its job; a <strong>warning</strong> means it works but deserves a human look. Every finding says what to do about it: select it in the Health column to read more. Nothing disables a redirect automatically, because almost no problem has a fix the plugin can safely apply."
msgstr ""

#: src/Infrastructure/WordPress/Admin/ListTable/ListScreenSetup.php:212
msgid "The source path (Redirect From) is checked for:"
msgstr ""

#: src/Infrastructure/WordPress/Admin/ListTable/ListScreenSetup.php:214
msgid "<strong>Reserved WordPress paths</strong> (warning): a source WordPress itself serves, such as <code>/wp-admin</code>, <code>/wp-login.php</code>, the other root <code>wp-*.php</code> files, <code>/xmlrpc.php</code>, or anything under <code>/wp-json</code>, <code>/wp-content</code> or <code>/wp-includes</code>. Such a redirect lies dormant while the path works, because redirects only answer 404s, but it takes over the moment that path breaks; for <code>/wp-admin</code> or <code>/wp-login.php</code> that locks you out of the dashboard. Keep it only if it is a genuine legacy URL."
msgstr ""

#: src/Infrastructure/WordPress/Admin/ListTable/ListScreenSetup.php:215
msgid "<strong>Published content at the source</strong> (warning): a published post or page of any public type already lives at the source path. Redirects only answer 404s, so the redirect lies dormant while that content is there. Unpublish the content if the redirect should fire; otherwise it is a fallback for when the content goes. Other content, such as an archive or a feed, is only found by Test."
msgstr ""

#: src/Infrastructure/WordPress/Admin/ListTable/ListScreenSetup.php:217
msgid "The destination (Redirect To) is checked according to its form:"
msgstr ""

#: src/Infrastructure/WordPress/Admin/ListTable/ListScreenSetup.php:219
msgid "<strong>Post ID destinations</strong>: the post must still exist, not be in the trash, and be published. Media attachments count as published."
msgstr ""

#: src/Infrastructure/WordPress/Admin/ListTable/ListScreenSetup.php:220
msgid "<strong>Relative path destinations</strong>: the path is resolved to a post of any registered post type, including via dated permalinks, and that post must be published. A path whose post was trashed is recognized even though trashing renames the slug. A path that resolves to no post at all - an archive, a rewrite endpoint, a page served outside WordPress - is not reported, because only a live request can judge it."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: the name of a WordPress filter, allowed_redirect_hosts; do not translate it.
#: src/Infrastructure/WordPress/Admin/ListTable/ListScreenSetup.php:223
#, php-format
msgid "<strong>External URL destinations</strong>: the host must be in the %s filter, or WordPress will refuse the redirect at request time and the visitor gets a 404."
msgstr ""

#: src/Infrastructure/WordPress/Admin/ListTable/ListScreenSetup.php:226
msgid "<strong>Possible loops</strong> (warning): a destination that is itself another redirect's source, with the hops leading back to where they started. A loop only runs while every source in it returns a 404 - any member serving real content keeps it dormant - so a person should judge it; break a cycle by re-pointing or disabling one member."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: a WP-CLI command, wp legacy-redirector test; do not translate it.
#: src/Infrastructure/WordPress/Admin/ListTable/ListScreenSetup.php:230
#, php-format
msgid "Whether a destination actually responds, and whether the redirect fires for a visitor, are live questions. The Test action answers both for the redirects you pick, as %s does from the command line."
msgstr ""

#: src/Infrastructure/WordPress/Admin/ListTable/ListScreenSetup.php:244
msgid "On multisite installations, each site manages its own redirects independently. Redirects created on one site do not affect other sites in the network."
msgstr ""

#: src/Infrastructure/WordPress/Admin/ListTable/ListScreenSetup.php:256
msgid "This site is installed below the domain root, so redirect paths are stored relative to the site's base URL rather than to the domain."
msgstr ""

#: src/Infrastructure/WordPress/Admin/ListTable/ListScreenSetup.php:257
msgid "In the Redirect From and Redirect To columns, paths are shown with a gray prefix giving that base URL. This makes clear that a path like <code>/hello-world</code> resolves against the site, not the domain root."
msgstr ""

